body:
## Bevy version

Bevy 0.11

## What you did

I attempted to create a rectangle with the alpha set to 0.5 on a black background.

## What went wrong

I expected the rectangle to be rendered with a final color of `rgb(128, 128, 128)`

The resulting color is `rgb(187, 187, 187)`, much brighter than what I expected.

## Additional information

I suspect that this is caused by some kind of sRGB color space correction.
As `rgb(187, 187, 187)` is directly the result of a linear to non-linear (sRGB) transformation applied on `rgb(128, 128, 128)`

One possible cause is the fact that Bevy prefers sRGB surfaces by default: https://github.com/bevyengine/bevy/blob/a0972c2b1bae30f705b2559026d1d4267e97abfd/crates/bevy_render/src/view/window.rs#L247
So there might be some kind of automatic conversion going on there.

One workaround is to make a post processing pass that "undos" the sRGB-ness of the image.
So by pre-`non_linear_srgb_to_linear`ing the surface, the final displayed image will simply reverse that operation instead of giving a sRGB corrected image.

I think this deserves an issue as it is not really the norm with other renderers (as far as I know) to give a sRGB corrected color.
For example, spawning a rectangle with the alpha set to 0.5 on a black background in PixiJS yields the expected result of `rgb(128, 128, 128)`.

[Here's the small test I made using PixiJS's playground](https://pixijs.com/playground?%3D%3D)

<details>
<summary>Here's the test code I used for Bevy</summary>

```
use bevy::prelude::*;

fn main() {
    App::new()
        .add_plugins(DefaultPlugins)
        .add_systems(Startup, setup)
        .run();
}

fn setup(mut commands: Commands, mut clear_color: ResMut<ClearColor>) {
    clear_color.0 = Color::BLACK;
    commands.spawn(Camera2dBundle::default());
    commands.spawn(SpriteBundle {
        sprite: Sprite {
            color: Color::rgba(1., 1., 1., 0.5),
            custom_size: Some(Vec2::new(300., 200.)),
            ..default()
        },
        ..default()
    });
}
```

