This is a serious problem,  all render results that involves transparency blending be wrong.

## My Test
Blend test image with white background.
### Original Image
![test_original_gridbg](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/38c4b46b-d6af-4cb0-9757-df31016094bb)
### Bevy result
![test_bevy](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/14d95f3d-e346-4c33-b088-4c13e538ed43)
### Code
``` rust
use bevy::prelude::*;


fn main() {
    App::new()
        .add_plugins(DefaultPlugins)
        .add_systems(Startup, setup)
        .run();
}

fn setup(
    mut commands: Commands,
    asset_server: Res<AssetServer>,
){
    let mut camera: Camera2dBundle = Camera2dBundle::default();
    camera.camera.clear_color = ClearColorConfig::from(Color::WHITE);
    commands.spawn((
        camera,
    ));

    commands.spawn((
        SpriteBundle {
            texture: asset_server.load("test.png"),
            ..default()
        },
    ));    
}
```

Experimenting with this issue, I have a minimal example to aid in diagnosis:

```rust
use bevy::prelude::*;

fn main() {
    App::new()
        .add_plugins(DefaultPlugins)
        .insert_resource(ClearColor(Color::srgba(0., 0.5, 0., 1.)))
        .add_systems(
            Startup,
            |mut commands: Commands| {
                commands.spawn(Camera2d);

                commands.spawn((
                    Sprite::from_color(Color::WHITE.with_alpha(0.5), Vec2::new(512., 512.)),
                    Transform::from_xyz(-256., 0., 0.),
                ));

                commands.spawn((
                    Sprite::from_color(Color::BLACK.with_alpha(0.5), Vec2::new(512., 512.)),
                    Transform::from_xyz(256., 0., 0.),
                ));
            },
        )
        .run();
}
```

When run, this produces the following image:

![Image](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/95aa1c44-4260-4732-8398-ae8e767811ef)

The white square is `rgb(187, 205, 187)`, the black is `rgb(0, 92, 0)`, and the background is `rgb(0, 128, 0)`.

Creating a similar image in GIMP produces the following image:

![Image](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/24cfc27c-9627-427e-8c5d-c42ead56a474)

The white square is `rgb(188, 204, 188)`, the black is `rgb(0, 92, 0)`, and the background is `rgb(0, 127, 0)`.

These values are close enough for me to say that GIMP and Bevy blend transparent colours in a similar way.

Now, comparing to Photopea (previously included as a comparison point):

![Image](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/49baeca3-eafa-4752-a448-af6acfc40679)

The white square is `rgb(128, 191, 128)`, the black is `rgb(0, 64, 0)`, and the background is `rgb(0, 127, 0)`.

As a final test, I created a white square in HTML/CSS:

![Image](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/742fc7d9-dd53-4e27-b424-d269409f04d8)

This gives results consistent with Photopea. The white square is `rgb(128, 191, 128)`, the black is `rgb(0, 64, 0)`, and the background is `rgb(0, 127, 0)`.

I wasn't expecting GIMP to _also_ give "incorrect" results, which made reproduction much harder than it needed to be. So, for anyone wanting to work on this bug, the following HTML document produces the same test pattern as the Bevy application, but with the "correct" colour values:

```html
<html>
  <head>
    <style>
      body {
        background-color: rgba(0, 127, 0, 1);
      }

      div#white {
        background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.5);
      }

      div#black {
        background-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.5);
      }

      div {
        float: left;
        width: 512px;
        height: 512px;
      }
    </style>
  </head>
  <body>
    <div id="white"></div>
    <div id="black"></div>
  </body>
</html>
```
